WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.510 align:start position:0%
 
let's get more from washington and speak  to democratic representative jake  ochencloth very good evening to you um  certainly a real day of reflection there  in in washington but paint the scene for  us if you can about your personal  experience that day there you were just  three days into the job having just been  elected um what did you see here where  were you and how quickly did you realize  there was something wrong  good evening and thanks for having me on  as you say a somber day here in  washington as we commemorate the one  year anniversary of the insurrection it  was my third day in office a year ago  the day before i had been touring the  capitol with my father and reflecting on  the  gravity of the institution  and  the day  of the sixth when i first knew something  was seriously wrong was when the cordon  of capital police at the perimeter of  the building started to stagger  i was in the marine corps for a number  of years before coming to congress and  trained in crowd control tactics and i  know that when according begins to  stagger  it's going to break because people can  get in between the police officers and  get behind them and when i saw it  stagger i knew the crowd was going to  turn from ugly to violent  and that was really a heart-palpitating  moment unfortunately it went downhill  from there and i can recall standing on  the house floor that night  on broken glass surrounded by national  guardsmen and women with assault weapons  as we  voted to certify the election results  your previous guest the national  security adviser the former national  security adviser  is right that it was a heinous day he's  wrong that the system worked as it was  supposed to the system is not supposed  to work this way the bulwark of a  democracy is the peaceful transfer of  power  and  as joe biden said this morning  you can't only love your country when  you win  losers need to peacefully accede to the  winner and that was violated a year ago  it undercut the integrity of our  democracy and we are still in the midst  of this constitutional crisis this is  not a historical event this is a current  event  well why do you think donald trump did  not intervene for so long  i think a number of things one is as mr  bolton said he rarely has a plan 15  minutes in advance of anything he says  so i think he's just baseline  incompetent and  unable to  to plan or project forward and number  two complete megalomaniac and and  narcissist who liked the idea of people  rioting on his behalf  this is an individual who has never once  in his life put anything above his own  self-interest and we should not have  expected him to do so on january 6th and  we should certainly not expect him to do  so in the future  i mean lots of lessons need to be  learned just want to ask you one in in  in the context really of you being a  former marine lots of people there were  veterans now why would they feel it  necessary to idolize donald trump in  such a fashion to go and you know break  the rules of law by breaking into this  building  january 6th was the cresting of years of  diss and misinformation of polarization  born of  media that has come to profit off of an  outrage as a service model of elected  officials who have thrown red meat to  their base instead of trying to solve  solve problems with one another it was a  culmination of a lot of different  cancers within the body politic  and some of them we know how to excise  so  first and foremost  donald trump  this is a uniquely unfit individual for  the presidency and must never be allowed  to hold power again  but also shoring up the separation of  powers and the checks and balances that  our framers  uh left us and we have a bill in  congress to do that they're protecting  our democracy act we need to pass that  bill guaranteeing access to the ballot  box  pushing back on partisan gerrymandering  getting dark money out of politics we  got a bill to do those things too the  freedom to vote act we got to pass that  bill in congress so  as we as we commemorate this one-year  anniversary it must not be a day solely  of reflection but a day of action we  need to pass the laws that can prevent  this from occurring  when you say that or you say donald  trump should never be allowed to hold  power again that's that's not in your  gift is it i mean there's there's still  a real prospect for 2024 is there not  that he could be  the nominee for the republican party  well it would have been when we  impeached him  in the house after the insurrection had  he been convicted in the senate he would  have been precluded from holding office  and we still have the 14th amendment  section 3. that section states that if  you swear an oath of the constitution  and then incite insurrection against the  u.s government you're unfit to hold  office in the future we can we can uh  levy  that amendment against him to bar him  from holding office these are  these are going to be controversial  moves to take for sure and yet we've got  to account for the clear and present  danger to our constitutional republic  that he represents and continues to  represent  uh in terms of the the errors that were  made that night um why did it take four  hours to secure the capital  why when it was quite clear what was  happening in pictures broadcast for  several hours the predicted march on the  hill the stated aims of some of the  participants  why was it so poorly defended  i think there's no question that there  were both intelligence and tactical  failures  emanating from leadership of capital  police through  some of the uh federal and state  intelligence agencies to coordinate to  be candid though i find those to be the  less compelling points of investigation  than the broader systemic issues of how  we got to an insurrectionist mob outside  of the capitol in the first place part  of the reason that january 6 was allowed  to happen as you state is that it was  unimaginable in america ahead of that  date that this could ever happen  and  what went from unimaginable to  inexorable needs to be the the true  cause for investigation how did we get  here as a democracy  and and yet there are still large  numbers of people in america who think  joe biden didn't win it's a form of  dysfunctional democracy and what message  does that send to the world really as  the us wants to hold autocrats to  account that you can't persuade your own  people that you held a lawful election  70 of republican voters to a certain  extent believe that  the election was stolen from donald  trump and as you say it is harder for us  to be a beacon of democracy and for us  to exert more leadership on the world  stage especially in an era when we're  competing against the chinese communist  party which ideologically is not  committed to free and fair elections uh  when we ourselves  cannot guarantee the peaceful transfer  of power through those free and fair  elections so  as we defend democracy abroad that work  begins at home  and just just very quickly  i was on that night the pictures  available subsequently from inside and  from those pressure points you talked  about you know the windows being broken  into the doors the the corridors where  there was literally a battle was there  not with law enforcement officials and  and the protesters many of your  colleagues have said they really thought  they were going to die that night is is  did did that cross your mind is that is  that the mood in the building  i was barricaded in my office i never  feel feared for my own physical safety  but then again  people weren't calling my name hunting  me down the hallways as they were for  some of my colleagues  well jake open class um good to have  your thoughts thank you very much indeed  there are u.s congress representatives  from massachusetts

